EVENT - Computah OS library
(generated from docs/API.md)

Signals and timers. Every waiting thread receives
every signal, so threads never steal events from
each other.

event.pull([timeout,] [name,] ...) -> name, ...

Waits for a signal and returns its name and
values, or nil after timeout seconds.

- timeout (number): seconds to wait at most.
  Without it, waits forever.
- name (string): a Lua pattern the signal name
  must match fully ("key_.*"), or an exact name.
- ...: filters; each non-nil filter must equal the
  signal's value at that position.
- Errors: interrupted when the user presses Ctrl+C
  while this program is in the foreground.

  local _, _, char, code = event.pull("key_down")             -- any key
  local _, addr, x, y = event.pull(5, "touch")                -- a touch within 5 s
  local _, _, from, port, _, msg = event.pull("modem_message", nil, nil, 42)  -- port 42 only

event.pullFiltered([timeout,] filter) -> name, ...

Waits for a signal for which filter(name, ...)
returns true.

  local ev = {event.pullFiltered(function(name, _, char) return name == "key_down" and char == 13 end)}

event.pullMultiple([timeout,] name, ...) -> name, ...

Waits for a signal with any of the given names
(exact names).

event.listen(name, callback) -> boolean

Calls callback(name, ...) for every signal with
this exact name, whatever thread is running.
Returns false if that callback was already
registered. If the callback returns false it is
removed.

Callbacks run between threads and must not block:
no event.pull, os.sleep or computer.pullSignal
inside them. Errors are passed to event.onError.

  event.listen("redstone_changed", function(_, address, side, old, new)
    print("side " .. side .. " is now " .. new)
  end)

event.ignore(name, callback) -> boolean

Removes a callback added with event.listen.
Returns whether it was registered.

event.timer(interval, callback[, times]) -> id

Calls callback() after interval seconds, times
times (default 1; math.huge repeats forever).
Timers run like listeners (must not block).
Returns an id for event.cancel.

  local id = event.timer(1, function() computer.beep() end, math.huge)

event.cancel(id) -> boolean

Stops a timer. Returns whether it existed.

event.push(name, ...) -> boolean

Queues a signal for this computer (same as
computer.pushSignal). Values may be nil, booleans,
numbers, strings and simple tables (copied).
Returns false if the queue (256 signals) is full.

event.onError(message)

Called with a message when a listener or timer
raises an error. By default it writes the message
to the kernel log (see dmesg). Assign your own
function to handle errors differently.

  event.onError = function(msg) io.stderr:write(msg .. "\n") end

event.handlers

Table {[name] = {callbacks...}} of the registered
listeners (read it, don't change it; use
listen/ignore).
